1. Test Automated Certificate Renewal

Let's Encrypt certificates carry a 90-day lifespan. Certbot installs a certbot.timer systemd service 
to automate renewals twice daily. Verify the dry-run execution:

sudo certbot renew --dry-run

Expected Output:

Processing /etc/letencrypt/renewal/app.yourdomain.com.conf
- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -
Account registered.
Simulating renewal of an existing certificate for app.yourdomain.com
Congratulations, all simulated renewals succeeded!

2. Verify HTTP-to-HTTPS Redirection via curl
Execute a HEAD request against the plain HTTP endpoint to confirm the 301 Moved Permanently response:

curl -I http://app.yourdomain.com

Expected Output:

 curl -I http://app.yourdomain.com
HTTP/1.1 301 Moved Permanently
Date: Mon, 10 Aug 2026 08:54:03 GMT
Content-Type: text/html
Content-Length: 166
Connection: keep-alive
X-Sucuri-ID: 20002
X-XSS-Protection: 1; mode=block
X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
Location: https://app.yourdomain.com/
Server: Sucuri/Cloudproxy
Alt-Svc: h3=":443"; ma=86400


3. Encrypted Ingress Test
Navigate to https://app.yourdomain.com in your web browser. Confirm the presence of the green connection padlock 🔒 indicating active TLS 1.2/1.3 transport encryption.
